steps.message:
One consequence of choosing `node --test`: the module has to be loadable outside a browser. So `settings.js` gets a small tail —

```js
if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) {
  module.exports = { resolveEnvironment, buildSettings, ENVIRONMENTS };
}
```

— which the browser ignores and Node uses. The `window.AppSettings` assignment is guarded by `typeof window !== "undefined"`, so importing the file under Node doesn't try to read `location.hostname`. That also matches the CommonJS convention already in `src/utils.js`.

Tests go in `test/settings.test.js` covering: each mapped hostname resolves to its environment, an unmapped hostname falls back to development, the fallback emits a warning, and `endpoints.login` composes base + path correctly. `package.json` gets `"scripts": { "test": "node --test" }` — still zero dependencies.

**Full scope:** new `settings.js`, new `test/settings.test.js`, edits to `app.js` (drop the constant, read from settings) and `index.html` (one script tag), and a `test` script in `package.json`.

Does this design look right? If so, I'll write it up as a spec at `docs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-16-settings-module-design.md` (uncommitted), run it through the review gates, and hand it to you to read before any code gets written.
